---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="When Does CSI Become Stale? - Solo Ghost"
  description="Why channel age, temporal correlation, spatial change, and receiver performance are different views of CSI aging."
>
  <article class="note">
    <header class="note-hero">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">TECHNICAL NOTE 02</p>
        <h1>When Does CSI Become Stale?</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">
          Channel age is a clock. Whether an old estimate still helps a receiver
          is a question about what changed and what the receiver needs.
        </p>
        <div class="note-meta"><span>SOLO GHOST</span><span>TECHNICAL NOTE</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">1 · THE AGE OF AN ESTIMATE</p>
        <p>
          A receiver estimates a channel from observations available at one
          time and may reuse that estimate later. As the channel evolves, the
          old estimate can become less useful. The elapsed time is its CSI age,
          but equal ages need not imply equal errors: motion, propagation,
          observation quality, and the receiver operation all matter.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          “Old” is a timestamp. “Stale” means that reuse is no longer adequate
          for a specified task.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">2 · THREE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS</p>
        <div class="compare">
          <div><strong>Temporal correlation</strong><p>How similar are channel samples across a time interval?</p></div>
          <div><strong>Spatial change</strong><p>How much has a channel direction or subspace moved?</p></div>
          <div><strong>Receiver consequence</strong><p>What happens to detection or decoding when the old state is reused?</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>
          These measurements can be related without being interchangeable.
          Correlation describes a statistical relationship. A spatial angle
          describes geometry. Error vector magnitude (EVM), bit error rate, or
          another receiver metric describes a consequence under a particular
          receiver and operating condition.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">3 · A TWO-ANTENNA THOUGHT EXPERIMENT</p>
        <p>
          Imagine an old, normalized receive-side channel direction
          <code> u₀ = [1, 0]ᵀ</code>. Later, its direction is 
          <code> u₁ = [0.8, 0.6]ᵀ</code>. Both vectors have unit norm.
          Projecting the new direction onto the old one captures
          <code> |u₀ᴴu₁|² = 0.64</code> of its directional energy; the
          remaining <code>0.36</code> lies outside that one-dimensional span.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now consider a different change: the direction remains
          <code> u₀</code>, but the complex channel coefficient changes. The
          directional leakage is zero even though a receiver using the old
          coefficient can still be wrong. Directional stability therefore
          cannot, by itself, certify that every part of CSI remains useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a geometric illustration, not a predicted EVM or a decision
          threshold. The receiver consequence depends on the transmitted
          symbols, noise, interference, equalizer, and other state.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section alt">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">4 · THE OBSERVATION IS NOISY TOO</p>
        <p>
          A measured channel estimate contains estimation error. At low
          observation quality, an apparent change in direction may partly be
          noise. A small observed change can also be uninformative when the
          measurement is unreliable. Repeating a calculation on the same
          noisy observation does not create independent evidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          The interpretation of any aging measurement should therefore state
          what was observed, its quality, the time or frequency separation,
          and the receiver task for which it is being used.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="note-section">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">5 · WHAT A USEFUL EXPERIMENT COMPARES</p>
        <p>
          A controlled evaluation can hold the received symbols and noise
          fixed while comparing receiver outcomes using an old estimate and
          an updated estimate. It can also report correlation and spatial
          measurements alongside the receiver metric. Such paired comparisons
          reveal when a geometric change matters to that receiver, while
          making clear which inputs were available only offline.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no universal CSI age at which every receiver fails. A useful
          answer specifies the channel setting, observation quality, receiver
          configuration, performance criterion, and measurement uncertainty.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          The engineering question is not simply “How much did the channel
          change?” It is “Does reusing this information still meet the task?”
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="note-footer">
      <div class="container narrow">
        <p class="section-label">SOLO GHOST</p>
        <p>
          This conceptual note uses an illustrative two-antenna example. It
          makes no claim of measured receiver improvement or a universal
          refresh rule.
        </p>
        <div class="footer-links">
          <a href="/notes/">All technical notes →</a>
          <a href="/research/">Research →</a>
          <a href="/ip/">Intellectual property →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .note-hero { padding: 112px 0 100px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .eyebrow, .section-label, .note-meta, code { font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .eyebrow, .section-label { color: var(--muted); font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  h1 { max-width: 900px; margin: 20px 0 28px; font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -.052em; font-weight: 600; }
  .subtitle { max-width: 720px; margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .note-meta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 22px; margin-top: 34px; color: var(--muted); font-size: .68rem; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .note-section { padding: 88px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .note-section.alt { background: var(--surface); }
  .note-section p { max-width: 760px; line-height: 1.75; }
  .note-section code { font-size: .88em; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  blockquote { max-width: 760px; margin: 34px 0; padding: 4px 0 4px 24px; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .compare { max-width: 820px; margin: 38px 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1px; background: var(--line); border: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .compare > div { padding: 24px; background: var(--bg); }
  .compare strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 8px; font-size: .95rem; }
  .compare p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .86rem; }
  .note-footer { padding: 72px 0 100px; }
  .note-footer > .narrow > p:not(.section-label) { max-width: 680px; color: var(--muted); }
  .footer-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 18px 28px; margin-top: 28px; font-size: .9rem; font-weight: 600; }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .note-hero { padding: 82px 0 72px; }
    h1 { font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 11vw, 3.5rem); }
    .note-section { padding: 68px 0; }
    .compare { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
